# Installation

> [!IMPORTANT]
> Custom item types are now available on *datalab*'s `main` branch, but are not
> yet part of a released version. Until they are, this plugin must be installed
> against [datalab-org/datalab](https://github.com/datalab-org/datalab)'s `main`
> branch (the development environment below already does this via
> `[tool.uv.sources]` in `pyproject.toml`).

## Development installation

We recommend you use [`uv`](https://astral.sh/uv) for managing virtual environments and Python versions.

Once you have `uv` installed, you can clone this repository and install the package in a fresh virtual environment with:

```
git clone git@github.com:{{ repository_namespace }}/{{ repository_name }}
cd {{repository_name}}
uv sync --all-extras --dev
```

You can activate `pre-commit` in your local repository with `uv run pre-commit install`.
This will call `pre-commit` automatically on every commit to check for code style and other issues, and will also be used in the CI.

## Installation in *datalab*

Declare the plugin in `plugins.toml` at the root of a *datalab* checkout. The
file is shared by native and Docker installations, and keeps plugin dependencies
out of *datalab*'s core `pyproject.toml`:

```toml
dependencies = [
    "{{ project_name }}",
]

[tool.uv.sources]
{{ project_name }} = { git = "https://github.com/{{ repository_namespace }}/{{ repository_name }}.git" }
```

For native development, the source can instead be an editable local checkout
(the path is resolved relative to *datalab*'s `plugins.toml`):

```toml
[tool.uv.sources]
{{ project_name }} = { path = "../{{ repository_name }}", editable = true }
```

Then install *datalab* and all declared plugins from its `pydatalab/` directory:

```shell
uv run invoke dev.install
```

This installs the plugin and, when the complete webapp source is available,
collects any custom Vue panels automatically.
{% if include_custom_panel %}
### Bundling the custom web UI panel

This plugin ships a custom Vue panel
(`src/{{package_name}}/webapp/{{item_class_name}}Panel.vue`). The *datalab*
webapp does not load plugin JavaScript at runtime. Native `dev.install` and both
Docker build profiles collect the panel before the webapp starts or compiles;
there is no follow-up collection command.

After changing the panel during Docker development, rebuild the development
frontend and its panel artifact from the *datalab* root:

```shell
docker compose --profile dev up --build app-dev
```
{% endif %}
### Docker development and production

With `plugins.toml` in place, these are the complete Docker workflows from the
*datalab* repository root:

```shell
docker compose --profile dev up --build
docker compose --profile prod up --build
```

Docker builds cannot access an editable path outside the *datalab* build
context. Use a Git source as shown above, or place the plugin checkout inside
that context.

### Other production deployments

The process of deploying plugins in production is similar to the local approach
above and depends on how you manage your *datalab* deployment. The recommended approach is to use the
[`datalab-ansible-terraform`](https://github.com/datalab-industries/datalab-ansible-terraform)
repository to manage your *datalab* deployment, which uses Ansible
to deploy *datalab* on a remote server. Provide the same `plugins.toml` to the
deployment so the plugin set remains explicit and reproducible.
